          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A rotation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+plane"} Euclidean plane
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            which leaves exactly one point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed
            or is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+map"} identity map
            = succeed "." do
              %em center of rotation
          %p
            A rotation of an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\geq 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            which either leaves exactly one
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperplane"} hyperplane
            (e.g. a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line"} line
            in dimension
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            = succeed "." do
              %em axis of rotation
          %p
            If we fix a point, this identifies the Euclidean space as a set with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            of all isometries fixing this point is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+group"} special orthogonal group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(V)
          %p
            Moreover, any choice of linear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            induces an identification of rotations with orthogonal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant"} determinant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+group"} special orthogonal group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(n,\mathbb{R})
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Recall that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(n,\mathbf{R})
            is a real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            of dimension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{n(n-1)}{2}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=3
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+angles"} Euler angles
          %p In 2d every nonidentity rotation sends any ray from the center of rotation to another ray from the center of rotation. The pair of the original ray and its image is constituting an ordered pair of rays, hence an oriented angle whose measure and orientation does not depend on a choice of the original ray; the corresponding signed measure is called the (oriented) angle of rotation. The rotation in 2d is determined by its center and the oriented angle of rotation. In 2d every nonidentity rotation may be represented as a composition of two reflections around axes which intersect in the center of rotation. Conversely, a composition of two reflections is a rotation iff the intersection of axes of reflection is exactly one point, or the axes are the same (when we obtain the identity). The angle of rotation is the double of the (measure of the) angle between the axes of reflections. Any two reflections with the same center and oriented angle between their axes determine the same rotation in 2d.
          %p
            In general, the orthogonal complement to the axis of rotation, passing through a given point on the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-2)
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            dimensions is determined by its
            = succeed "-axis" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-2)
          %p
            The group of rotations is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(3,\mathbf{R})
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(3)
          %p
            %strong> Euler’s theorem on rotations
            \: a composition of rotations of a sphere is a rotation of a sphere. It is trivial that it is an isometry (when viewed as a transformation of the space), the nontrivial part of the theorem is that there is a
            = succeed "-hyperplane" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-2)
          %h2#relation_to_quaternions_in_3d_and_4d Relation to quaternions in 3d and 4d
          %p
            Consider the vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
